Threads vs ExecuorService

// Threads are managed manually
// ExecutorService manages a pool of threads and tasks automatically, introduced in Java 5 with java.util.concurrent package

public class ThreadsVsExecutorService {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Using Threads directly
        Thread thread1 = new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Thread 1 is running");
        });
        thread1.start();

        // Using ExecutorService to manage threads
        ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
        executorService.submit(() -> {
            System.out.println("Task 1 is running");
        });
        executorService.submit(() -> {
            System.out.println("Task 2 is running");
        });
        executorService.shutdown();
    }
}

| Aspect               | Thread               | ExecutorService               |
| -------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Thread creation      | Manual               | Automatic from pool           |
| Task reuse           | No                   | Yes                           |
| Task result          | No                   | Yes (via Future)              |
| Exception handling   | Manual               | Automatic with Future         |
| Parallelism          | You manage           | Managed by pool               |
| Queuing              | No                   | Yes, extra tasks queued       |
| Lifecycle management | Manual               | Managed (shutdown, terminate) |
| Recommended          | Simple one-off tasks | Real-world production apps    |

Both support Runnable and Callable, but ExecutorService is more powerful and flexible for managing multiple tasks and threads efficiently.
Callable allows exceptions via futureTask.get() while Runnable does not.
